On Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:24:19 -0700, Pat Maddox wrote:
> Interesting, so I just want to make sure - the normal git-send-email
> feature rewrites dates, right? Because I assume the mailing lists are
> not full of commits with dates from a while back. Basically there's a
> difference between the message delivery time, and the commit authorship
> time.

Yes. I checked the git send-email shipped with our git 2.56.0
(/usr/local/libexec/git-core/git-send-email). It does three things
with Date:

- It drops any Date the input carries. The header parser keeps other
  headers but skips Date ("elsif (!/^Date:\s/i && ...)"), so the
  author date that format-patch wrote never goes out.

- It writes a fresh Date for every message from a clock it starts
  before sending: "$time = time - scalar $#files;". For N files that
  is now minus N-1 seconds.

- It advances that clock one second per message
  ("format_2822_time($time++)"), so a series goes out in strictly
  increasing seconds, and the last message is dated about now.

It also does not carry the author date anywhere else. When the author
differs from the sender it adds an in-body "From:" line, but no
in-body Date. So the author date is lost in transit, and git am records
the send time as the author date. That is why kernel and git history
mostly show patches dated when they were mailed, not when they were
written. Fact: the code above. Inference: the claim about kernel and
git history, which I could not check directly, because lore.kernel.org
refused my fetch.

> I want to do what mailing lists do. So double check the behavior of the
> open source lists and make sure we match.

The list servers themselves (Mailman, mlmmj, public-inbox) pass Date
through as sent. MTAs such as Postfix and sendmail add a Date only when
one is missing. So the Date a reader sees is the one the sending client
set, and for patches that client is git send-email. Inference: this is
from those projects' documented behavior, which I did not test here.

Our delivery script stands in for both the client and the MTA, so
matching send-email is what matters. "[PATCH] libexec/mail: Date each
message when it is delivered" [1] matches it on the points that count:

- The commit's author date is dropped and replaced with the time the
  message is posted.
- A series is dated in one-second steps, in the order it was sent.
- git am then records the posting time as the author date.

There is one difference. send-email knows how many messages it will
send, so it starts N-1 seconds in the past and ends at now.
maildir-deliver.sh gets one message per call and never knows N, so it
starts at now and steps forward. Each delivery is also dated at least a
second after the newest message already in the maildir. A series of N
can therefore be dated up to N-1 seconds in the future, where
send-email's is up to N-1 seconds in the past. send-email has no such
floor across runs. Two runs in the same second can share timestamps,
and their messages then interleave.

I suggest keeping the patch as it is. A few seconds of skew either way
does not matter to a reader. The floor also keeps a series in order
against the reply that follows it in the same pass, which send-email
does not promise. mail-send.sh, which runs git send-email with this
script as its sendmail, still gets increasing dates in order. The
script replaces send-email's Date with its own, which is never earlier.

If you would rather match send-email exactly, I can do that in a v2.
Delivery would take the whole series in one call, so it knows N and can
back-date the series to end at now. Let me know and I will send it.

